# PART 4 — THE BOOKSHELF AND ARCHIVED NOTEBOOKS

## 4.1 Physical Bookshelf Experience

Create a separate interactive 3D bookshelf.

When users click the bookshelf, transition to a bookshelf view.

Archived notebooks should be arranged like real physical books standing vertically on shelves.

This is a critical visual requirement.

Books must:

- Stand next to each other.
- Have visible spines.
- Display their titles on their spines.
- Have realistic thickness and proportions.
- Have distinguishable cover colors.
- Be arranged naturally on the shelves.

Do not display archived notebooks as digital cards.

They must look like actual books stored on a physical bookshelf.

## 4.2 Bookshelf Interactions

Users should be able to:

- Browse archived notebooks.
- Select a book.
- Pull a book out of the shelf.
- Open and read it.
- Restore it to the writing table.
- Permanently delete it after confirmation.

Provide realistic book-selection and opening animations.

## 4.3 Archiving Behavior

When a user archives a notebook:

- Remove it from the active table.
- Preserve all notebook content.
- Move it into the archive collection.
- Display it on the bookshelf.

Archiving must never delete content.

Restoring a notebook should return it to the active table with its previous appearance and arrangement information where possible.

