# PART 18 — GOOGLE AUTHENTICATION AND CLOUD SYNCHRONIZATION

Users must be able to access their notebooks from anywhere.

Implement real Google account authentication.

Support:

- Google sign-in.
- Secure sign-out.
- Persistent sessions.
- Account-specific data.
- Cloud storage.
- Automatic synchronization.
- Image storage.
- Cross-device access.

## 18.1 Automatic Saving

Users should not need to manually save their work every time they write.

Automatically save:

- Typed text.
- Handwriting strokes.
- Drawings.
- Photographs.
- Image positioning.
- Page layouts.
- Bookmarks.
- Notebook settings.
- Application settings.
- Last opened page.
- Notebook arrangement.

Show a discreet saving or synchronization indicator.

Clearly distinguish saving, saved, and synchronization error states.

Avoid excessive network requests for every individual stroke.

Use efficient batching, debouncing, and safe persistence strategies.

## 18.2 Offline Support

Where practical, support offline editing.

If the user temporarily loses connectivity:

- Preserve local changes.
- Allow writing to continue.
- Queue changes safely.
- Synchronize when connectivity returns.
- Handle conflicting changes carefully.

Never silently discard unsynchronized journal content.

## 18.3 Security and Privacy

Every user's data must remain private.

Implement:

- Secure authentication.
- Proper authorization.
- Account-level data isolation.
- Secure image storage.
- Appropriate database access policies.
- Safe session handling.

One user must never be able to access another user's private journals.

