# FINAL PRODUCT VISION

Daybook should feel like a personal study room that belongs to the user.

The table holds the notebooks they are currently using.

The bookshelf holds the notebooks they have archived.

Each notebook has its own physical appearance, binding, cover, pages, bookmarks, and personal history.

Users should be able to pick up a notebook, open it, select a real-looking pen, write naturally, draw anywhere, attach photographs, switch colors, bookmark important pages, and close the book.

The application should remember exactly where they stopped.

The environment should feel realistic and beautiful, but the writing process should remain simple, immediate, and distraction-free.

There should be no unnecessary complexity.

There should be no generic productivity dashboard.

There should be no fake functionality.

There should be no visual compromises that make the notebook feel like an ordinary digital document.

**Build a complete, responsive, persistent, production-ready digital journaling web application that recreates the experience of owning and using real physical notebooks.**

The ultimate goal is simple:

**Make users feel that they are sitting at their own desk, writing in their own personal notebook, surrounded by their collection of books and memories.**

Build Daybook as a real product, not another note-taking website.